course: world
lesson: 185
title: Some, all, none and "not all"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Small words do big jobs. "All" means every single one, "none" means not one, "some" means at least one, and "not all" means at least one isn't.
explain: The classic mix-up is hearing "not all" as "none": "Not all teachers are strict" doesn't mean no teacher is strict. Mr Brown still gives looks that could boil water.
explain: Two things in the same big group are not the same thing: cats are animals and dogs are animals, but cats are not dogs. Max's sister: "All carrots are food, all chips are food, so carrots are chips? I wish, Max."
explain: Groups inside groups do work: if all bloops are razzies and all razzies are lazzies, then all bloops are lazzies, even with made-up words. Draw a circle inside a circle inside a circle and you can see it.
explain: And an "all" only goes one way: "all the boys in my class like football" says nothing about who else likes football.
fact: Two things being in the same big group doesn't make them the same: cats and dogs are both animals, but cats are not dogs.
fact: If all A are B and all B are C, then all A are C; it works even with made-up words, like circles inside circles.
fact: "Some" means at least one; it doesn't say how many, and it doesn't mean all.
fact: "Not all" means at least one isn't; it doesn't mean none.
fact: "Not everyone liked the film" means at least one person didn't like it; others still could have.
fact: Not all birds can fly: penguins, ostriches, emus and kiwis are birds that can't, but all birds have feathers.
fact: In everyday talk "some" often hints "not all", but it doesn't promise it: if every sweet in the bag is strawberry, "some are strawberry" is still true.
fact: A puppy is a young dog, so "no dogs allowed" includes puppies.
fact: "Not every kid got a sticker" doesn't say which kids missed out.
fact: "All the boys in my class like football" doesn't mean everyone who likes football is a boy in my class.
fact: Words people use: "I wish" means "sadly that's not true"; "sick" (young people) means great; "bare" (London) means loads of; "fam" (London) means mates; "howay" (North East England) means "come on"; "gadgie" (North East) means a man; "footy" (UK, Australia) means football; "yeah nah" (Australia, NZ) means no.
exercise: Max: "All cats are animals. All dogs are animals. So all cats are dogs." What is wrong? | Being in the same big group doesn't make them the same | Cats aren't really animals | Dogs aren't really animals | Nothing; the reasoning is right
exercise: "All bloops are razzies. All razzies are lazzies." Are all bloops lazzies? | Yes, whatever the words mean | No; made-up words can't be reasoned about | Only some bloops are lazzies | No bloops at all are lazzies
exercise: How can you draw the bloops, razzies and lazzies? | A circle inside a circle inside a circle | Three circles side by side | Two circles that just touch | Three circles that never meet
exercise: Mum: "Some of the kids in your class like broccoli." Jake: "So everybody likes it except me?" What does "some" mean? | At least one | Everybody | Everybody except one | Nobody
exercise: Ellie: "Not all teachers are strict." Her friend: "So no teachers are strict?" What is right? | At least one isn't strict; others may be | No teacher is strict at all | Every teacher is strict | Exactly half of the teachers are strict, no more
exercise: After the film Leo says, "Not everyone liked it." Sam: "So nobody liked it? I liked it." What does "not everyone" mean? | At least one person didn't like it | Nobody at all liked the film, not one | Everybody in the cinema liked it a lot | Exactly half of the people liked it
exercise: Ruby: "All birds can fly. Penguins can't fly. So penguins aren't birds." What is wrong? | The first sentence is false | The second sentence is false | Penguins are really fish | Nothing; the reasoning is right
exercise: Which of these birds can't fly? | Penguins, ostriches, emus and kiwis | Sparrows, robins, crows and barn owls | Eagles, swans, ducks and geese | Pigeons, parrots, gulls and swifts
exercise: Which rule really is true for all birds? | All birds have feathers | All birds can fly | All birds live in trees | All birds can sing
exercise: Mum: "Some of the sweets in the bag are strawberry." Every one is strawberry. Did Mum say something false? | No; "some" doesn't promise "not all" | Yes; "some" always means "not all" of them | Yes; "some" means exactly half | No, because she meant "none"
exercise: A shop sign says "No dogs allowed." Amy: "My puppy's allowed, he's a puppy, not a dog." Is she right? | No; a puppy is a young dog | Yes; puppies are a different animal | Yes; the sign means grown dogs only | No, because puppies are cats
exercise: Teacher: "Not every kid in the class got a sticker." Oscar cries: "So I didn't get one!" What is true? | It doesn't say which kids missed out | It means Oscar got no sticker at all, sadly | It means nobody in the class got a sticker | It means only Oscar missed out on one
exercise: Jade: "All the boys in my class like football. Sam likes football. So Sam must be a boy in my class." What is wrong? | Other people like football too | All football fans are boys | Sam can't like football | The first sentence must be false
exercise: What is the opposite of "all teachers are strict"? | At least one teacher isn't strict | No teacher is strict | All teachers are kind and never strict | Most teachers are strict
exercise: Which pair means the same? | "Not all are strict" and "some aren't strict" | "Not all are strict" and "none of them are strict" | "Some are strict" and "all are strict" | "None are strict" and "some are strict"
test: Max's sister: "All carrots are food, all chips are food, so carrots are chips? I wish, Max." What does she show? | Being in the same group doesn't make two things the same | Carrots really are chips | Chips aren't food | Max was right about cats
test: Mia: "OK, that's sick actually." If all glimps are flarks and all flarks are blops, is every glimp a blop? | Yes | No | Only some of them | You can't tell with made-up words
test: Jake: "Bare people like broccoli? Not me, fam." Mum only said "some". What did Mum actually say? | At least one kid likes broccoli | Loads of kids like broccoli | Every single kid but Jake likes it | No kid likes broccoli
test: Ellie: "Mr Brown gave me a look that could boil water." Her friend thought "not all teachers are strict" meant none are. Can Mr Brown be strict? | Yes; "not all" leaves room for some strict ones | No; "not all" means none are strict | Only if every teacher is strict | No teacher can ever be strict
test: A gadgie in Newcastle: "Not everyone liked the match, like." His mate: "So nobody did? Howay, I loved it!" Who misread it? | The mate; "not everyone" isn't "nobody" | The gadgie; he should have said nobody | Both of them misread it, the gadgie and the mate | Neither; the two mean the same
test: What would happen to "all birds can fly" if you found just one bird that can't? | It would be shown to be false | It would still be true for all birds | It would turn into "some birds are fish" | Nothing; one bird doesn't count
test: Tom: "You said some! You tricked me!" Mum: "Every one being strawberry still includes some." Is Mum's sentence true? | Yes; "some" doesn't promise that others are different | No; "some" means not all of them | No; "some" means exactly half | Yes, but only if one isn't strawberry
test: The shopkeeper: "Nice try, love. A puppy's a dog. Small, cute, very much a dog." What trick did Amy try? | Changing a word's meaning to dodge a rule | Hearing "some" as "all" in the shop's sign | Running an "if" backwards | Attacking the shopkeeper
test: Oscar's friend: "Bro, you've got a sticker. A shiny one. You cried for nothing." Why did Oscar cry for nothing? | "Not every kid" didn't say it was him | The teacher said Oscar got no sticker today | Stickers can't be shiny, so it doesn't count | "Not every" means that nobody got one at all
test: An Aussie kid: "All the boys in my class like footy. Sam likes footy, so Sam's a boy in my class? Yeah nah." Why "yeah nah"? | The "all" goes one way only | Sam can't like footy | All footy fans are in that class | Only boys can like footy
test: In a cartoon a sign says "No cats". A kitten strolls in saying, "I'm a kitten, not a cat!" and the guard waves it through. Put the cartoon right. | A kitten is a young cat, so the sign includes it | Kittens are a different animal from cats | The sign only means big cats like lions | Kittens are allowed to go anywhere
exam: It is given that all bloops are razzies and that all razzies are lazzies. Select the response consistent with the premises. | All bloops are lazzies | No bloops are lazzies | Some bloops are not lazzies | No conclusion may be drawn
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion "not all teachers are strict"? | At least one teacher is not strict | No teacher is strict | Every teacher is strict | Precisely half of all teachers are strict
exam: Identify the flaw in the argument that, since all cats are animals and all dogs are animals, all cats are dogs. | Membership of a common group does not establish identity | The premise that cats are animals is false | The premise that dogs are animals is false | The argument contains no flaw
exam: Ascertain whether a notice reading "No dogs allowed" applies to a puppy. | It applies, since a puppy is a young dog | It does not apply, since a puppy is not a dog | It applies only to puppies of large breeds | It applies only when the puppy is unaccompanied
